  internationally  is that uh france is coming under  significant pressure you know  there are large countries now some of  which i just mentioned you know calling  for the usual things boycotts of french  goods  uh demonizing the whole state not just  not just the current government though  the current president but the whole  foundation of the state  in france um in that situation it seems  to me that there has  arisen in our time a certain tendency of  other european and western states to try  to duck  the whirlwind to sort of hope that the  whirlwind passes over their house  we saw something similar in 2005 and six  with the danish cartoons controversy  even other scandinavian countries wanted  to slightly step away from denmark  they didn't want to be in the same  boycotts as denmark was already in  and i think this is an enormous shame  and a missed opportunity as well as  a missed opportunity to assert a  principle of solidarity across  the free world i guess what i'm saying  douglas is that because the president  has put the very soul of the republic  at the heart of this not saying that  muslims are in any way incompatible with  republican french values but the the  islamist view of the world is  definitely incompatible with it that's a  pretty big battle he's taking on that  yes of course he's not the first french  leader to take on that battle uh um  uh emmanuel vales when he was prime  minister recently in france took on  the same battle asserted some of exactly  the same principles that mr macron has  what's interesting about it this time is  that macro appears to be acting and not  just  talking uh a a few weeks ago ion hersey  ali in the magazine was rightly  skeptical about whether this would just  be more words from the french president  but in the weeks since then we have seen  a number of actions  uh the withdrawal of the french  ambassador to turkey quite rightly after  the outrageous  um misrepresentation of the french  president's remarks  by president edwan in turkey  calls uh by the french for further  strictures against the turkish  government including  uh being cast out of the trade  cooperation agreements that it has with  the eu and then there has been  other progre um progressive developments  including the closure  of the bank accounts indeed the banning  of certain extremist groups in france  and then most remarkably in some ways  the french president's  uh call for an indeed ordering of a  doubling of french border forces  and then his assertion of two things in  particular one  he says that when he comes to the  european council meeting  in december he will be calling for first  of all  a rethink of the schengen  agreements which allow the free movement  of course of people across borders once  they are in europe and secondly  a strengthening of external borders this  must be on his mind in particular  because  of the um of the fact that the attacker  in nice who has been detained for  killing three people outside the church  uh is a young tunisian man who entered  europe in september through the italian  island of lampedusa which i reported  from for the spectator  in 2015-26 for years people have been  able to tell european leaders and have  said  if you don't if you have an entirely  porous external border  and free movement within the borders of  europe  this is a security nightmare that will  keep on  emerging now previously it has been  principally central european eastern  european leaders who have made that  point  in recent days there's been another  flare-up of tension between chancellor  merkel and her counterpart in austria  the the austrian chancellor sebastian  kurtz over exactly this issue  but to have uh uh that fight in the past  tended to be a sort of east europe  versus west europe point  to have emmanuel macron  saying the same things that are being  said in central and eastern europe  indeed in mediterranean europe  is a very significant development and it  leaves effectively  chancellor merkel alone  what do you say final question douglas  to to  the most cynical point which is it's  just all politics  that uh the center right in france is on  its back  the center left is untears the socialist  parties do not manage to rebuild itself  the mr melenchon runs the left he's  never going to get very far  mr macron has concluded that the only  real threat to his re-election is marine  le pen  and he's moved his tanks onto her lawns  there is a possibility it's it's it's  it's politics as usual  uh of course as you know in the last uh  presidential election in france in some  ways it was a sort of  fluke or at least a very small  percentage chance that meant  that uh that macro and le pen went  through  as they did because the the vote was  effectively split  uh in quarters in france and  uh the the calibration that macro  macro is making could just as easily if  it was totally cynical go the other way  in some ways  um it's true he needs to take some of  those le pen votes but he needs to take  votes from a number of directions uh i  myself  think that you know one can't read into  the hearts of politicians  uh what we can do is judge their words  and judge whether they act on their  words  and that is what makes this particular  episode unusual  it's not just being words that are  actually actions you know  um calling for diplomatic sense against  the turkish government for its  outrageous colonies against the french  republic  is a stepping up uh er  actually closing groups not just talking  about closing uh  groups that teach extremism and  encourage extremism but actually closing  them  is not just words its actions so perhaps  this is some kind of corner i'm very  skeptical as you know of the  tendency to look for tipping points and  inflection points but in the long battle  of the french republic against islamic  extremism  what we're going through at the moment  is definitely some form of progress  [Music]  you

